            <td data-label="Who it genuinely fits">{item.fitsWho}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Neither track is objectively better. The honest deciding question is not "which one sounds more
    impressive on LinkedIn," because they all use real design judgment. It is: does the behaviour and
    usability of a digital product pull you in more, or does visual communication for brands pull you in more,
    or does the business "why" behind a design decision energise you as much as the "what." Answer that
    honestly before the word "design" answers it for you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="freelance">Freelance vs full-time: the honest income math</h2>

  <p>
    UI/UX design is reasonably well suited to freelance and contract work, because a flow, prototype, or
    design-system deliverable does not need the designer physically present, but it is a harder freelance
    market than graphic design because clients expect research rigour, not just visual output.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Full-time, in-house or product company</div>
      <ul>
        <li>More predictable income and a clearer growth ladder into senior, lead, or product-design roles.</li>
        <li>Faster access to real research infrastructure, user data, and testing budgets than most freelance clients can offer.</li>
        <li>Less control over project selection, but stronger structured feedback and mentorship early on, where a team actually invests in it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Freelance or independent</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Genuinely global client access through platforms and direct outreach, with a low floor early on, roughly Rs 550-1,150 an hour for most active freelancers.</li>
        <li>A real ceiling for designers who build a specific niche, a case-study-backed portfolio, and repeat clients, well past what most salaried entry roles pay.</li>
        <li>Requires running an actual small business: scoping research within a client's budget, pricing, contracts, and collections, not just design skill.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Many strong UX designers move between the two: building research and stakeholder-handling skill inside a
    job first, then taking that judgment independent once a specific niche and a few repeat clients are
    already proven.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-fits">Who this path genuinely fits</h2>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Genuine fit</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">You are more curious about why users get confused than about how a screen looks</div>
      <p>The exciting first-concept moment is a small, front-loaded slice of the work. Most weeks involve research logistics, stakeholder alignment, and iteration. If that rhythm sounds like real problem-solving rather than drudgery, the field fits your work style, not just your visual taste.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Genuine fit</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">You are willing to build research and data judgment AI cannot template</div>
      <p>Because no licence protects this title, and because AI now handles the purely templated production tasks, genuine fit includes the discipline to build real research, testing, and data-reading skill, not just Figma fluency.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Genuine fit</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">You can sustain a modest income runway while a real niche forms</div>
      <p>Entry pay for generic UI/UX work is modest, and the junior applicant pool is genuinely crowded right now. Genuine fit includes financial and family readiness for a harder-than-expected first stretch while a specific niche, research, product design, or AI-interface work, actually forms.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="who-pause">Who should think twice before committing</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Warning sign</th>
          <th>What is actually true</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>Choosing UI/UX design mainly because a bootcamp ad promised fast, high-paying remote work</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">Real income and shortlist odds in this field come from a genuinely tested portfolio and a specific niche, not from a two-month bootcamp and a Dribbble account. If the appeal stops at "easy and remote," the entry-level competition and AI-commoditised template work will feel disproportionate to the reward.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>Not checking a specific bootcamp or course's real placement or alumni outcomes before enrolling</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">With no accreditation body policing quality and dozens of bootcamps now competing for enrolments, this single check matters more than course-ad promises, testimonial screenshots, or a friend's casual recommendation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>Assuming Figma fluency alone will carry a whole career</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">Research depth, data literacy, and the ability to use AI as a speed layer are what actually separate a low-paid, high-rejection generic-portfolio outcome from a stronger one, inside the exact same field.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>Assuming UI/UX design is inherently more secure or higher-paying than adjacent fields, without comparing real numbers</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">Product design and UX research roles frequently out-pay a generic UI/UX title at the same experience level. If the interest is genuinely broad, comparing tracks honestly before committing to a specific label matters more than the label itself.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    None of this means these career-switchers or students cannot succeed in UI/UX design. It means the
    specific reason behind the choice may need a second look, and building toward a genuine research or
    product-focused niche from the start, rather than drifting into generic template work, matters more here
    than it did a few years ago.
  </p>

