        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Technical software fluency alone does not fully explain the pay gap either. A designer who can explain a
    usability finding clearly to a non-design stakeholder, defend a flow decision with data rather than
    opinion, or use an AI tool to speed up a first draft and then meaningfully improve on it consistently gets
    pulled into higher-visibility work than an equally skilled peer who only executes briefs literally.
    Communication and data reasoning are not soft add-ons here; they decide who gets handed the
    strategy-facing project versus who stays on routine production work.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>This is really the whole game:</strong> the "UI/UX designer" title decides which room you walk
    into, but a genuine <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong>, research and product judgment plus the
    ability to explain and defend it with data, built on top of it decides whether you unlock stronger income
    opportunities inside that room, or spend years competing against hundreds of near-identical bootcamp
    portfolios for the same low-value tasks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ceiling">How to actually raise your income ceiling</h2>

  <p>
    A fair salary table only tells you where most people land, not where the strongest outcomes actually come
    from. UI/UX design's income ceiling is not fixed by the field; it is set by how far past "screen executor"
    a person is willing and able to move.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      The clearest ceiling-raising moves inside this field are: developing a specialist niche like UX research,
      accessibility, or AI-interface design, where genuinely few designers are competent end to end, moving
      into a product-design role where you sit in growth and prioritisation conversations rather than only
      executing briefs, adding data literacy that lets you connect a design change to a business metric, and
      pairing design skill with a genuinely useful AI workflow that lets you take on more research-heavy or
      specialist work instead of more routine screen production.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of these are guaranteed outcomes, and none happen from software skill alone. They happen for
      designers who pair craft depth with visible, tested proof, business fluency, and enough judgment to
      price and sell their own expertise, not just perform it for a fixed low rate.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    For anyone genuinely leaning toward the freelance or independent route, price a project for the outcome it
    delivers the client, a validated flow, a usability report with clear recommendations, not just the hours
    behind it, and build a small, repeatable service menu, a UX audit, a specific research sprint, rather than
    an open-ended custom-everything offer that is hard to scale. Early clients usually come from referrals and
    a visible body of tested work, not cold outreach, so building a habit of asking happy clients for the next
    introduction matters more than it seems in the first year. It is also worth registering under Udyam early
    once work becomes regular, since it is free, and checking current GST turnover thresholds before assuming
    registration can wait, both of which catch independent designers off guard more often than software costs
    do.
  </p>

  <p>
    On AI specifically, the realistic path is staged, not a single leap. Right now, the useful move is
    learning to work alongside AI-assisted design tools so first-draft wireframing and layout variations take
    a fraction of the time they used to. As adoption matures over the next few years, the bigger opportunity
    shifts toward owning the research and verification layer: testing whether users trust an AI-generated
    interface decision, catching a usability problem a generated flow misses, and using the freed-up time to
    take on more research-heavy or specialist work instead of more routine screen production.
  </p>

  <h2 id="alternatives">When a nearby path fits better</h2>

  <p>
    UI/UX design is not automatically the strongest option for everyone drawn to "design" or "tech-adjacent
    creative work." Two adjacent paths genuinely beat it for a real segment of readers, for specific,
    concrete reasons, not just as padding.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Consider UX research instead if...</div>
      <ul>
        <li>You are more energised by interviewing people and finding patterns in messy qualitative data than by producing screens.</li>
        <li>You want a track that is genuinely under-supplied in India right now relative to demand, especially for AI-feature testing, rather than one crowded with bootcamp graduates at entry level.</li>
        <li>Pay for dedicated research roles (roughly Rs 8-16 LPA) already runs ahead of generic UI/UX titles at similar experience, and the automation-exposure profile is lower.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Consider product design instead if...</div>
      <ul>
        <li>You are as interested in why a feature should exist and what it should do to a business metric as in how it looks and behaves.</li>
        <li>You want the fastest-scaling pay track inside the broader design field (roughly Rs 9-20 LPA entry to mid), because product companies budget this work as a business function.</li>
        <li>You are comfortable defending a decision with retention or revenue data in a leadership review, not just a usability finding.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Neither alternative makes plain UI/UX design a bad choice. Both are simply better-paying, less-crowded
    versions of the same underlying interest for a specific kind of person, and worth genuinely comparing
    before locking into a bootcamp built purely around visual interface work.
  </p>

